This principle holds for a wide range of physical systems, from classical mechanics to quantum field theory. In the limit of very large actions, the system's behavior can be approximated by classical mechanics (Hamilton's principle). In quantum mechanics, the path integral formulation shows that all possible paths contribute to the total probability amplitude, weighted by a phase factor related to the action. As the action approaches zero (or some fundamental quantum scale), quantum effects become more prominent, and the classical path is no longer the dominant contributor. The boundary conditions are crucial, as the variation \(\delta q(t)\) is usually taken to be zero at the initial and final times, \(\delta q(t_1) = \text{and} 
